<comment datatype='string'>The &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2469&quot;&gt;eutP&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2468&quot;&gt;eutQ&lt;/a&gt; genes of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-28901&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Salmonella enterica&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; are located next to each other in the &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; utilization operon. Bioinformatics analyses show that EutQ belongs to the cupin superfamily, which includes a wide variety of enzymes such as carbohydrate-binding isomerases, flavonoid-binding dioxygenases and nuclear factors.
&lt;p&gt;
A &amp;Delta;&lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2468&quot;&gt;eutQ&lt;/a&gt; strain released &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETALD&quot;&gt;acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; into the medium during growth on &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=GLYCEROL&quot;&gt;glycerol&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16585748&quot;&gt;Penrod06&lt;/a&gt;]. When the mutant was grown under anaerobic conditions with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; as the main carbon source (a small amount of glycerol was also included) and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-14&quot;&gt;tetrathionate&lt;/a&gt; (40 mM) as the terminal electron acceptor, growth stop after consumption of the glycerol. Growth inhibition was not observed if a gene encoding &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-1.2.1.10&quot;&gt;EC 1.2.1.10, acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (acetylating)&lt;/a&gt; was introduced and expressed at a high level, indicating that the growth inhibition was due to accumulation of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETALD&quot;&gt;acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/26448059&quot;&gt;Moore16&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The authors suspected that the &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2468&quot;&gt;eutQ&lt;/a&gt; gene may encode an acetate kinase. To test this hypothesis, several other genes encoding acetate kinase were introduced into the mutant, resulting in each case in restored anoxic growth of the mutant on ethanolamine and tetrathionate. The opposite was also true - overexpressing &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2468&quot;&gt;eutQ&lt;/a&gt; in a &amp;Delta;ackA strain corrected its growth defect [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/26448059&quot;&gt;Moore16&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2469&quot;&gt;eutP&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=STM2468&quot;&gt;eutQ&lt;/a&gt; genes were cloned with a poly His tag and expressed in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and the recombinant proteins were purified to homogeneity. The purified proteins were assayed &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; in the opposite direction, and both catalyzed the production of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACET&quot;&gt;acetate&lt;/a&gt; and ATP when provided with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETYL-P&quot;&gt;acetyl phosphate&lt;/a&gt; and ADP. When either protein was assayed in a coupled reacction with purified EutD and acetyl-CoA, both formed ATP and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACET&quot;&gt;acetate&lt;/a&gt;, indicating that they are indeed acetate kinases [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/26448059&quot;&gt;Moore16&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
While the location of the proteins is not known yet, the fact that they are specifically encoded by the &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon suggests that they are localized within the metabolosome.
</comment>
